# -*- mode:org; coding:utf-8 -*-
#+TITLE: write-atomic-file-concurrent-rename-macos-windows — protocol-write-atomic-file-contention fails natively on macOS + Windows
#+STATUS: TODO
#+PRIORITY: P2
#+TYPE: bug
#+DEPENDS: -
#+BRANCH: fix-write-atomic-file-concurrent-rename-macos-windows

* Context

Found 2026-08-03 by the NEW native CI lanes =test:alfe:{macos,windows}=
(pipeline #701, sha f9820f8) — the first time the alfe FiveAM suite ran on
the real macOS/Windows runners rather than only the Linux docker lane.

Result: =PROTOCOL-WRITE-ATOMIC-FILE-CONTENTION= (file-protocol-tests.lisp)
is the ONLY failing test on both:
  - macOS   566/567 (1 fail)
  - Windows 556/561 (same test; its per-thread-error asserts count several)
  - Linux   green (567/567) — never exercised the real-hardware concurrency.

* Problem

The test stress-tests =write-atomic-file=: 16 writer threads × 50 writes each
race =write-atomic-file= onto ONE target while a reader loops 1000× asserting
the file is never torn/missing. On macOS + Windows:

  - writers signal "couldn't rename": =write-atomic-file= writes a temp sibling
    then =uiop:rename-file-overwriting-target= (POSIX rename / Windows MoveFileEx
    REPLACE_EXISTING). Under concurrent renames onto the same target WHILE the
    reader holds it open, the rename transiently fails (Windows sharing
    violation; macOS contention). No retry.
  - reader signals FILE-DOES-NOT-EXIST / SIMPLE-STREAM-ERROR:
    =read-file-as-string= does =(probe-file path)= THEN =(with-open-file …)= — a
    TOCTOU: a writer's rename lands between the probe and the open, so the open
    hits a momentarily-absent/locked target. No retry.

* Not a real-usage bug

The live file-IPC protocol is SINGLE-writer-per-file across SEPARATE processes:
the CAD process writes status/stdout/stderr; the alfe process writes
stdin/control. Nothing writes the same file concurrently, so real CAD runs are
unaffected (BricsCAD Windows was GREEN). This is the stress test asserting a
stronger atomicity-under-concurrent-multi-writer property than POSIX-vs-Windows
rename can cheaply guarantee.

* Proposal — one of (pjb to decide the intent)

(a) HARDEN the protocol primitives (defensive; makes them robust + passes the
    test): bounded retry loop with a tiny backoff on transient failures —
    =write-atomic-file= around the rename (Windows sharing violation / macOS),
    =read-file-as-string= around the open (re-probe/re-open on a transient
    not-found after probe said present). Windows file sharing is inherently
    racy; retries are the standard remedy.

(b) RELAX the test: concurrent-multi-writer-to-one-file is not a real scenario;
    reduce the writer count, tolerate a bounded number of transient
    rename/open failures, or gate the contention test to Linux with a note.

* Acceptance

- =test:alfe:macos= and =test:alfe:windows= go green in CI.
- If (a): the retry is bounded (never an unbounded spin) and Linux stays green.
